  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records establish the as-cast FCC/B2 lamellar structure and deformation behavior of the exact AlCoCrFeNi2.1 alloy, show that 600 °C annealing can cause consequential phase decomposition in a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy, identify L12-containing microstructures as thermally stable strengthening structures, and directly connect FCC/B2 interface character with stress concentration, dislocation transfer, and interfacial cracking. Collectively these findings point toward thermally tuning ordering and interfaces to alter ductility. However, none identifies the specific non-monotonic 600–750 °C ductile-brittle-ductile transition, near-stoichiometric L12 ordering within the FCC domains, jagged-boundary formation, or the coupled APBE/cross-slip and boundary-tip instability mechanism. Reaching the later result would therefore require a substantial new mechanistic and temperature-specific insight."
